t to underground gardens as a substrate. \",\"They chew it up\",[106],\"—leaves, flowers, etc.—not to eat directly but as “compost.” \",\"They collect plant material\",[106],\". Here’s what they do: \",\"Attini\",[231],\" and their relatives in the tribe \",\"leafcutter ants\",[106],\"Yes, some ants literally farm fungi. The classic example is \",[433],[434],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":138,\"_103\":435},[106],[437,438],{\"_95\":443},{\"_95\":439},[440],[441],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":442,\"_103\":-7},\"Yeah. Some ants—especially leafcutter ants in the Americas—are full-time fungus farmers. They cut leaves, haul them underground, and use the leaf bits as compost for cultivating a specific fungus that’s their main food source. It’s not a casual thing, either—they’ve been running that system for millions of years, and the fungus species they grow can’t survive without them. In turn, the ants depend on the fungus entirely. Mutual dependency dialed up to “forever marriage.”\",[444],[445],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":150,\"_103\":446},[106],[448,449],{\"_95\":454},{\"_95\":450},[451],[452],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":453,\"_103\":-7},\"Yes. Several ant species, mainly in the tribe Attini, cultivate fungi for food. They gather plant material, place it in underground chambers, and grow specific fungal species on it. The fungus produces nutrient-rich structures the ants eat.\",[455],[456],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":162,\"_103\":457},[106],[459,460],{\"_95\":465},{\"_95\":461},[462],[463],{\"_71\":101,\"_101\":464,\"_103\":-7},\"Yes, and they’re weirdly good at it. Certain ant species, mostly in the leafcutter group, have been running little underground fungus farms for millions of years—long before humans figured out agriculture. They haul leaves or flowers back to their nests, not to eat directly, but to mulch and feed to a carefully cultivated fungus that serves as their actual food source. They even weed, fertilize, and protect the crop from pests using bacteria they grow on their own bodies. Meanwh